text: |-
  Subarachnoid hemorrhage - cerebellar vermis
  ME Report, Id. at 3-4
  Subarachnoid hemorrhage - parietal regions
  ME Report, Id. at 3-4
  53 Hz cavity-mode dominant peak (30-60 Hz
  band)
  UCCU Audio, 11 cameras
  +14.1 dB cavity-to-structural energy ratio
  IMG_6368 (10.1m)
  Low energy above 500 Hz (no Cooper Injury
  Range signal)
  UCCU Audio
  104 microsecond rise time (4.9x faster than
  PETN)
  Canon XA55 (Video 15)
  Cooper acoustic coupling effect (foam on thorax
  amplifies injury)
  Cooper et al. 1991, J Biomech
  Courtney thoracic-to-brain TBI mechanism
  Courtney & Courtney 2009, Med Hypotheses
  .. BAN
  2/5
  Requires retrograde vertebral artery flow - projectile
  is above posterior fossa; needs vertebral artery
  disruption + retrograde hemorrhage
  1/5
  Difficult to explain - parietal lobes supplied by carotid
  system (MCA), not vertebrobasilar; requires CSF
  spread from distant source
  3/5
  Possible if bullet enters thorax - could excite
  Helmholtz mode, but less violent pressure discontinuity
  Inan explosive decompression
  2/5
  Unusually high for ballistic event - 25:1 energy ratio
  favoring cavity mode is extreme for a decelerating
  projectile
  3/5
  Consistent - rifle produces no high-frequency stress
  waves; absence of 1-3 kHz energy is expected for
  ballistic event
  1/5
  Cannot explain - no ballistic mechanism produces a
  rise time faster than PETN detonation
  0/5
  Not applicable - no surface detonation on chest;
  acoustic coupling mechanism does not apply to
  ballistic events
  2/5
  Possible but weaker driver — bullet entering thorax
  creates pressure transient, but much lower magnitude
  -than exolosive event
  9 4/5
  Courtney thoracic vascular surge mechanism —
  thoracic pressure event creates vascular surge to brain
  (validated experimentally, 2009/2014)
  4/5
  Courtney thoracic vascular surge - systemic vascular
  surge affects entire cerebral vasculature including
  MCA/parietal territory
  • 5/5
  Direct Helmholtz prediction — jet transition from tissue
  to air inside lung = explosive decompression at FRC
  volume (-3 L)
  5/5
  Predicted by internal excitation - jet + detonation
  deposits energy directly inside cavity; cavity-mode
  dominance expected
  3/5
  Consistent — micro-charge may not generate
  detectable 1-3 kHz at distance; foam coupling may
  filter high frequencies
  2/3
  Consistent with high-VOD explosive -
  detonation with shock-focused geometry can exceed
  PETN rise time
  5/5
  Direct match - battery/device padding on chest
  functions as Cooper's foam coupler, amplifying stress
  wave transmission
  5/5
  High-magnitude thoracic pressure event - detonation
  + jet = large thoracic pressure transient; predicts
  widespread cerebrovascular injury
  SC +2
  SC +3
  SC + 2
  SC +3
  EQUAL
  sc
  SC +5
  SC +3
